<h2>The scheme and the defence</h2>
<p>The prosecution&rsquo;s case concerns investor money gathered through a portfolio-management arrangement operating without the registration that such activity requires. The collected corpus — about Rs 12.96 crore — is the subject of the EOW&rsquo;s investigation into how it was solicited, deployed and lost to the investors who parted with it.</p>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s defence was positional: he was a shareholder, and he had resigned as director. The submission, in essence, was that whatever the company did, his exit placed him outside the ring of responsibility. Justice Pushkarna rejected the premise — the resignation did not negate his role during the period before it. The investigation legitimately extends to what each functionary did while the scheme was collecting money, and a later resignation cannot retrospectively launder that period.</p>

<h2>The doctrinal frame</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Anticipatory bail is exceptional.</strong> Following <em>Srikant Upadhyay v. State of Bihar</em>, pre-arrest protection is not the default mode of criminal procedure; it is an extraordinary power for exceptional cases.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Economic offences are a class apart.</strong> <em>P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement</em> — with the line of cases it carries — treats financial crime as deliberate, profit-driven and systemically corrosive, warranting a stricter bail approach.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custodial interrogation has value.</strong> Where funds must be traced across entities and accounts, the investigating agency&rsquo;s need to question the accused in custody is a legitimate consideration — <em>Sumitha Pradeep</em> as applied in <em>P. Krishna Mohan Reddy</em>.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Balancing survives.</strong> <em>Siddharam Satlingappa Mhetre</em> remains the counterweight: liberty is not forfeited by accusation. But the balance in investor-fraud cases tilts where the money is untraced.</div>
</div>

<h2>What this means for directors and shareholders</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s EOW docket is dense with collective-investment and portfolio schemes, and the exposure of individual functionaries follows function, not title. Three distinctions matter in practice. First, the period of office: liability tracks conduct while in the chair, so the precise dates of appointment and resignation — and the Form DIR-12 trail proving them — should be assembled before any court appearance. Second, the role actually played: signing authority, investor-facing representations and control over accounts weigh far more than the bare description of shareholder or director. Third, the money: a functionary who can show he neither received nor routed scheme funds stands on materially better ground, because the custodial-interrogation rationale weakens where there is no trail to extract.</p>
<div class="note">An anticipatory bail dismissal decides the mode of investigation, not guilt. But in economic offences it often precedes arrest and a contested regular bail hearing, where the twin themes — gravity and fund-tracing — return. Preparation for that next stage should begin the day the pre-arrest plea fails.</div>

<h2>The current legal map</h2>
<p>The FIR invokes the IPC because the conduct predates 1 July 2024. For later conduct, the corresponding provisions are Sections 316 and 318 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (criminal breach of trust and cheating) with Section 61 (criminal conspiracy), and the anticipatory bail jurisdiction is Section 482 BNSS — under which this very application was decided.</p>
